." cp(1)
.TH CP 1 "February 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cp \- copy files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp
.RI [ \-R " [\-H|\-L|\-P] ]
.RI [ \-f | \-i | \-n ]
.RI [ \-p [ a|mode ] ]
.RI [ \-l | \-s ]
.RI [ \-b " bufsize ]"
.RI [ \-\-sparse = MODE ]
.RI [ \-\-reflink = WHEN ]
.RI [ \-\-remove-destination ]
.RI [ \-\-atomic-replace | \-\-no-atomic-replace ]
.RI [ \-\-progress ]
.RI SOURCE ... DEST
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cp
copies regular files and, with
.BR \-R ,
directories.
.PP
Destination resolution:
.TP
- If DEST exists and is a directory, each SOURCE is copied to DEST/basename(SOURCE).
.TP
- If multiple SOURCE operands are provided, DEST must be an existing directory.
.TP
- If one SOURCE is provided and DEST is a non-directory path, SOURCE is copied to DEST.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-R , " \-r" , " --recursive"
Recurse into directories.
.TP
.B \-H
When recursing, follow symlinks specified on the command line only.
.TP
.BR \-L , " --dereference"
When recursing, follow all symlinks.
.TP
.BR \-P , " --no-dereference"
Copy symlink objects themselves. This is the default recursive behavior.
.TP
.BR \-f , " --force"
Force overwrite and suppress human-readable warnings. Exit status still reports errors.
.TP
.BR \-i , " --interactive"
Prompt before overwrite. On non-tty input, default answer is "no" unless
.B --interactive-default=yes
is set.
.TP
.BR \-n , " --no-clobber"
Never overwrite existing files.
.TP
.BR \-p " [a|mode]"
Preserve attributes. Bare
.B \-p
preserves mode, owner/group, and timestamps.
.B \-p a
preserves all available attributes (mode/owner/time/links/xattr/acl/flags).
.B \-p mode
preserves mode only.
.TP
.BR \-a , " --archive"
Archive mode: equivalent to recursive copy with full metadata and hardlink preservation.
.TP
.BR \-l , " --link"
Create hard links instead of copying file contents.
.TP
.BR \-s , " --symbolic-link"
Create symbolic links instead of copying file contents. The link target text is the original SOURCE argument.
.TP
.BR \-b , " --buffer-size=" SIZE
Set I/O buffer size. SIZE accepts decimal or hexadecimal numbers and suffixes such as k, m, g, kb, mb, gb.
.TP
.BR "--preserve=" LIST
Preserve a comma-separated list: mode, ownership, timestamps, links, xattr, acl, flags, all.
.TP
.BR "--sparse=" MODE
Sparse policy: auto (default), always, or never.
.TP
.BR "--reflink=" WHEN
Copy-on-write clone policy: never, auto, or always.
When set to
.B always
and clone is unsupported, copy fails.
.TP
.B --remove-destination
Remove an existing destination path before opening/writing the replacement.
This replaces symlink paths themselves rather than writing through them.
.TP
.B --atomic-replace
Enable temporary-file copy followed by rename for overwrite operations (default).
.TP
.B --no-atomic-replace
Disable atomic replacement and write directly to destination.
.TP
.B --progress
Print per-file copy progress lines to standard error.
.TP
.BR "--interactive-default=" yes|no
Set non-tty default answer for
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B --help
Display usage and option summary.
.TP
.B --version
Display version and exit.
.SH RECURSION AND SYMLINK SEMANTICS
For recursive copies:
.TP
- Default is physical traversal (
.BR \-P ).
.TP
- 
.B \-H
follows command-line symlinks only.
.TP
- 
.B \-L
follows all symlinks.
.PP
Directory traversal skips
.B .
and
.B ..
and tracks visited directory inodes to avoid symlink-induced cycles.
.SH METADATA PRESERVATION
With
.B \-p
or
.BR \-a ,
.B cp
preserves requested metadata on a best-effort basis.
.PP
If owner/group changes require privilege and fail, the copy still completes unless a fatal I/O error occurs. Xattr/ACL/flags preservation degrades gracefully on unsupported platforms.
.SH SPECIAL FILES
With archive/all-preserve modes,
.B cp
recreates FIFOs and device nodes when permitted. Socket nodes are reported and skipped.
.SH ATOMIC REPLACE
When overwriting regular files,
.B cp
can write to a temporary file in the destination directory, fsync it, and rename it over the destination. On failure, temporary files are cleaned up.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
All requested copies succeeded.
.TP
1
One or more paths failed to copy, or a fatal write/fsync/rename error occurred.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
cp file1 file2
Copy one file.
.TP
cp file1 file2 dir/
Copy multiple files into a directory.
.TP
cp -R -P srcdir dstdir
Recursive physical copy.
.TP
cp -R -L srcdir dstdir
Recursive logical copy (follow symlinks).
.TP
cp -a srcdir backup/
Archive copy with metadata and hardlink graph preservation.
.TP
cp -l source dest
Create a hardlink.
.TP
cp -s source dest
Create a symlink to source.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
"destination for multiple sources must be a directory"
Create destination directory first or pass a single source.
.TP
"hard link ... Cross-device link"
Hard links require source and destination on the same filesystem.
.TP
"symbolic links unsupported on this target"
The current target runtime does not expose symlink creation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mv (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR stat (2),
.BR link (2),
.BR readlink (2),
.BR mknod (2)
